        "local_order_2adic_pattern": "aligned_positive",
        "number_of_components": "1",
        "modulus_size": "small"
      },
      "human_solution": "Since gcd(2, 5) = 1, the base is a unit modulo 5. The multiplicative order of 2 modulo 5 is d = 4. If 2^n \u2261 -1 (mod 5) then squaring gives 2^(2n) \u2261 1, so d | 2n, while d does not divide n. Therefore n must be an odd multiple of d/2 = 2. Direct computation gives 2^2 \u2261 4 \u2261 -1 (mod 5), so the exponent d/2 does produce -1. The least such n is therefore 2.",
